course: adult-life
lesson: 29
title: Screens, diets, sofas and late nights
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Grown-ups make rules for children about screens, food and sleep, and then often break the same rules themselves, because habits are hard at any age.
explain: Phones and apps are built to grab attention; food, weight and exercise worry many adults; and tired grown-ups snore, nap and stay up too late.
explain: Picture a Tuesday in Toronto: Dad says "No phones at the table" and checks his own under it, Mum skips dessert because she's "being good", and after you go to bed they both watch TV until midnight and yawn all the next day.
explain: None of this makes them bad parents; it makes them people.
explain: Children can notice it kindly, laugh about it together and sometimes help everyone keep the rules.
fact: Families make phone rules so meals are for talking, and adults find them hard to keep because phones are built to grab attention.
fact: Doomscrolling means reading bad news on a phone, one story after another, often late at night; it usually makes people feel worse.
fact: On social media, "likes" can start to feel like a score of how popular you are, but they measure attention, not a person's worth; most apps are for 13 and over.
fact: Grown-ups limit children's screen time because too much can harm sleep, mood and play, though screens affect adults' sleep and mood too.
fact: A diet means eating less or differently to lose weight or feel healthier; some diets are sensible and some are fads that do not last.
fact: "I'm being good today" about food is just a phrase; eating a biscuit does not make anyone bad.
fact: Many people pay every month for a gym and then stop going, because starting a habit is easy and keeping it is hard.
fact: Running and other exercise release chemicals in the brain that lift mood, and free weekly parkruns are open to all ages.
fact: Swear words are strong words people say when hurt, angry or shocked; adults try not to swear in front of children and often feel embarrassed when they slip.
fact: Snoring is the noise of air rattling soft parts of the throat in sleep; couples sometimes sleep apart just to get a good night's rest.
fact: A big meal and a warm room make many adults sleepy, and "I was only resting my eyes" is a classic joke for a nap.
fact: Some TV programmes are made for adults because they are scary, violent or about grown-up problems, and evenings are when tired parents finally relax.
fact: Staying up too late makes adults tired the next day, and children's sleep matters even more for growing and learning.
exercise: Dad says "No phones at the table" — then checks his own under the table. Why? | Phones grab adults' attention too and habits are hard | Dad is allowed because he is the one who pays the phone bill every month | The rule is only for people under ten | His phone is not really a phone
exercise: What can you kindly do when Dad breaks his own phone rule? | Remind him nicely that the rule is for everyone | Grab his phone off the table and throw it in the bin | Check your own phone under the table too | Tell him he is a terrible father
exercise: Mum in bed late, scrolling and sighing: "The world's a mess." What is she doing? | Doomscrolling bad news on her phone | Reading a happy story for fun before bed | Writing a list for the shops | Checking the weather for tomorrow
exercise: How does doomscrolling usually make people feel? | Worse, not better | Calm and sleepy | Happy and hopeful | Clever and rested
exercise: Big cousin checks her phone again and again: "Only twelve likes." What is she worried about? | That not enough people liked her post | That her phone battery is low | That she has twelve messages to answer | That her phone costs too much
exercise: What do likes on social media really measure? | Attention, not how much a person is worth | How kind and how clever a person really is | How many real friends a person has | How much money a person has
exercise: "Five more minutes on the tablet, then off" — said by a grown-up on the phone all evening. Why do they limit your screen time? | Too much screen time harms sleep, mood and play | Tablets break if they are used for too long | Screens only hurt adults and never children at all | They want the tablet for themselves
exercise: Mum after dinner: "No pudding for me, I'm on a diet." What does she mean? | She is eating less to lose weight or be healthier | She is allergic to all puddings | She has already eaten pudding in secret | The doctor has banned her from eating sugar for ever
exercise: A colleague refusing biscuits: "No, I'm being good today." Is eating a biscuit bad behaviour? | No, food is not good or naughty | Yes, biscuits are only for bad people | Yes, sweet food is against the rules | No, but only on Saturdays
exercise: Dad pays every month for a gym he hasn't been to since January. Why does this happen to so many people? | Starting a habit is easy but keeping it is hard | Gyms close down after January every single year | The gym does not let him in | Paying the fee counts as exercise
exercise: Neighbours run at 6 a.m. in the cold, red-faced and puffing. Why do they do it? | To stay fit and feel calmer and happier | Because they are late for the bus | Because someone is chasing them | To get warm after a cold shower
exercise: Dad stubs his toe and says a word he tells you never to say. What happened? | He swore because he was hurt | He spoke in a foreign language | He made up a new word for toe | He said a word from the dictionary
exercise: If you hear a grown-up swear, what should you do? | Not repeat it; knowing a word is not using it | Shout it at school the next day so everyone hears | Write it on the whiteboard | Tell him he is in big trouble
exercise: Mum: "Your father snores like a tractor, I slept in the spare room." What does it mean? | His loud snoring kept her awake | He was driving a tractor in his sleep | Mum and Dad are splitting up | Mum likes the spare room bed better
exercise: Grandad asleep on the sofa after Sunday lunch: "I was only resting my eyes." What really happened? | He fell asleep for a nap | He had something in his eyes | He was counting sheep for fun | He was pretending to be a statue
exercise: Mum: "This programme is for after your bedtime." Why? | It is made for adults and may be scary or about grown-up problems | It is broadcast only in other countries | It is far too boring for children to understand or to sit through | It is not allowed for anyone to watch
exercise: Why does sleep matter for children? | It helps them grow and learn | It makes them shorter | It keeps them from getting hungry | It is only needed in winter
test: Your sister says "Grandad wasn't asleep, he said he was only resting his eyes!" Put her right. | He was napping; "resting my eyes" is a joke for falling asleep | He really was just closing his eyes for a second and heard every word | He was doing an eye exercise | He was listening to the radio
test: Overheard: "Signed up to the gym in January, 40 quid a month, been twice. Twice!" What does the speaker mean? | He keeps paying but hardly ever goes | He goes to the gym twice every day | The gym is free for him | He has saved a lot of money
test: Ava, 11, cries because her post got "only 9 likes" and her friend's got 50. What is true? | Likes measure attention, not how good a person is | Ava is less liked as a person than her friend is | The friend with 50 likes is a better person | Ava should post more until she gets 100
test: Dad, hopping round the kitchen: "Ow, son of a— sorry, sorry, didn't mean that." What happened? | He nearly swore after hurting himself and felt embarrassed | He was telling a story about his son | He was singing a song in the kitchen while he was cooking tea | He was talking to the dog
test: Mum yawning at breakfast: "Shouldn't have watched three episodes." What is the link? | Staying up late watching TV made her tired | Watching TV always makes adults hungry in the morning | The programme was about yawning | She did not watch anything last night
test: You say "Five more minutes" back to Mum who's been on her phone two hours. She laughs: "Fair point." Which is a good idea? | A screen-free game or walk together | Both of you staying on screens all night | Hiding her phone in the bin | Shouting that she is a hypocrite
test: Auntie: "I can't, I'm on this new juice diet, just juice for three weeks." Gran: "Faddy nonsense." What does Gran think? | It is a fad diet that will not last | It is a sensible healthy plan | Juice is the best food for adults | Auntie should drink more juice
test: Your mate: "My mum and dad sleep in different rooms cos of the snoring, they must be getting divorced." Is that right? | No, sleeping apart because of snoring is normal | Yes, separate rooms always means a divorce is coming | Yes, snoring is a sign of an argument | No, they are sleeping in a tent
test: Mum at midnight, scrolling: "Floods, war, prices up again..." What would most help her? | Putting the phone down and getting some sleep | Reading more news until she feels better | Turning up the brightness of the screen | Sharing every story with the family
test: Cousin Tom says "Eating cake makes you naughty." What should you tell him? | Food is not good or bad behaviour; sweets are for sometimes | He is right, so you should never eat cake again in your life | Cake makes you naughty only on weekdays | Only grown-ups get naughty from cake
test: Dad after his first parkrun: "That nearly killed me, but I feel brilliant now." Why does he feel brilliant? | Exercise releases chemicals that lift mood | He won a big prize for coming first | He did not really run at all | He is pleased the run was cancelled
exam: Identify the principal reason for which parents restrict the duration of their children's use of electronic screens. | Excessive use is detrimental to sleep, mood and play | Electronic devices are damaged by prolonged operation | Children are incapable of operating such devices | Screens are harmful exclusively to adults
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the practice known as "doomscrolling". | The prolonged reading of distressing news, which tends to worsen mood | The careful study of weather forecasts prior to undertaking a long journey | The writing of letters of complaint to newspapers | The deletion of all applications from a telephone
exam: It is given that a person discontinues attendance at a gymnasium while continuing to pay the monthly subscription. Ascertain the most probable explanation. | Habits are easily begun but difficult to sustain | The gymnasium prohibits attendance after January | The subscription constitutes a form of exercise | Payment is obligatory only for absent members
exam: Determine what is thereby asserted when an adult who has been asleep declares "I was only resting my eyes". | A humorous denial that the adult had fallen asleep | A medical statement concerning an eye condition | A request that the lights be extinguished | A report that the adult was awake throughout
